# The files of tinyssh (third_party/tinyssh, vendored unmodified) that the
# build replaces with ours: upstream's file is left out and ours, in
# user/ports/tinyssh, compiled in its place. Each of ours starts with
# upstream's notice and says what changed and why. tinyssh.mk's
# tinyssh-check (in `make check`) and every build check the hashes with
# tools/checkoverrides.py, as for musl: a new release that changed one of
# these files stops the build until someone has read upstream's change,
# brought ours up to date and recorded the new hash here.
#
# One override per line: the upstream file (relative to third_party/tinyssh),
# the SHA-256 of the upstream file ours was written against, and ours.
#
# Left out of the build entirely (tinyssh.mk says why): channel_fork.c,
# channel_forkpty.c, channel_drop.c, loginshell.c, dropuidgid.c, logsys.c,
# main_tinysshd_makekey.c, main_tinysshd_printkey.c, the lib25519,
# libntruprime and lib1305 variants, the feature probes and _tinysshd-speed.c.

# The session's child: one of init's sessions (jamos_session_open) instead
# of openpty, fork, login_tty and execve of a shell.
channel.c de5ce435762220def586a46a37a3d0b7cf6703151af1c08d63c36fc53db9fedf user/ports/tinyssh/channel.c
# The host key's signature in a helper started with posix_spawn, not fork.
subprocess_sign.c af49532652441979531b2e55d1fbb3d2ad9f4f9c74a758d5192fb1a8c39573a3 user/ports/tinyssh/subprocess_sign.c
# The key check in a helper started with posix_spawn, not fork; the keys
